  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Combating the West African trade in Threatened Vultures and their parts for belief-based use</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In West Africa, illegal trade in vulture parts for belief-based practices is the leading cause of population declines. Nigeria is a source and transit country for the trade in vulture parts. Three of the six vulture species found in Nigeria are critically endangered. To combat the illegal trade in vulture parts, this two-year grant will support: a survey of major wildlife markets to identify the quantity, value, and type of vulture parts being sold; a survey of perceptions of consumers, traditional healers, and traders of wildlife products; identification and promotion of viable and sustainable plant-based alternatives to the use of vulture parts in belief-based uses; post-surveys to assess any decrease in traded vulture parts as a result of the use of alternatives; development of an action plan and manual for enforcement agencies to investigate and monitor the trade in vulture parts; training for enforcement agencies on legislation, investigations, and monitoring of the trade in vulture parts; development of a national action plan on illegal wildlife trade to integrate international policy frameworks into national legislation; and a public awareness campaign on the negative impacts of wildlife trafficking.</narrative>
  </description>
